WEBVTT

00:00:00.405 --> 00:00:03.443
Journey twenty-three: recover from errors.

00:00:03.443 --> 00:00:08.652
Anyone who meets a missing, forbidden, failed or maintenance page sees a

00:00:08.652 --> 00:00:13.065
clear page and a way forward. This run takes the journey from

00:00:13.065 --> 00:00:15.453
seventy-nine percent to complete.

00:00:16.311 --> 00:00:21.031
In this demo, Legacy Financial was once listed as Legacy Financial

00:00:21.031 --> 00:00:26.181
Group. Someone follows a link they were sent under the old name, and the

00:00:26.181 --> 00:00:27.183
listing opens.

00:00:28.041 --> 00:00:32.397
When an owner changes the business name, the listing's address now

00:00:32.397 --> 00:00:36.819
follows it, and the old one is kept. The old address answers with a

00:00:36.819 --> 00:00:40.713
permanent redirect, and is never given to another business.

00:00:41.674 --> 00:00:46.222
An address that is no page of Jubilee shows the not-found page, with

00:00:46.222 --> 00:00:47.962
Back to browse and Search.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:52.605
And now the server says so before the app even loads.

00:00:52.605 --> 00:00:57.206
The app's host checks each page load against the app's addresses and

00:00:57.206 --> 00:01:01.941
asks the API: four oh four for a missing page or a listing that is not

00:01:01.941 --> 00:01:06.609
public, four oh three for an area that is not yours, and three oh one

00:01:06.609 --> 00:01:07.692
for an old link.

00:01:08.583 --> 00:01:13.247
While Jubilee is being updated, every page load answers five oh three,

00:01:13.247 --> 00:01:17.777
with Retry After, and this page: a static copy, with no script, that

00:01:17.777 --> 00:01:21.975
says when Jubilee is back and that nothing you've sent is lost.

00:01:22.714 --> 00:01:27.303
If the app itself cannot be served, the host answers five hundred with a

00:01:27.303 --> 00:01:31.764
static copy of the error page: Try again, Get help, and a reference to

00:01:31.764 --> 00:01:34.186
quote, which leads support to the log.

00:01:35.069 --> 00:01:39.667
Joanna opens the owner's workspace, but she owns no listing.

00:01:39.667 --> 00:01:44.189
The page tells her plainly, and she presses Ask for access.

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:50.026
The request is passed to the team. Nothing is granted.

00:01:51.064 --> 00:01:56.115
With the request, the verification team is now e-mailed: the page, who

00:01:56.115 --> 00:02:01.094
asked and when. A repeated press, or more than five in an hour, sends

00:02:01.094 --> 00:02:02.032
nothing more.

00:02:02.887 --> 00:02:07.855
Tests prove each criterion: API tests for the slugs, the statuses and

00:02:07.855 --> 00:02:13.039
the e-mail, host tests for every page-load status, and browser tests for

00:02:13.039 --> 00:02:15.559
the redirects and the static pages.

00:02:16.412 --> 00:02:21.693
Journey twenty-three is complete. Nothing new needs setting on Azure.

00:02:21.693 --> 00:02:26.820
Left for the owner: check the statuses after deploying, and run the

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:30.188
visual, responsive and accessibility sweeps.
